<h1>Video 098 — Java thread pools (Executor framework)</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: Multithreading Enhancement  Part- 4|| java thread pools</p>
<p>ASR decode: keep subing = keep writing; thread pole / thre = thread pool / thread; executor / executors = ExecutorService / Executors; fixure / fixed thre pool = newFixedThreadPool; runable / rable = Runnable; cable / callable = Callable; color = caller; written type = return type; retri = retrieve; DGA / dura / durga = Durga; pan = Pavan; Sur = Suresh; legend = listen; TR catch = try-catch.</p>
<h3>00:05 — Topic: Thread pools (Executor framework)</h3>
<p>Board: Thread pools (Executor framework).</p>
<h3>00:45 — Connection-pool analogy (JDBC)</h3>
<p>Creating a DB connection every time, using it, closing it → performance and memory problems. Instead: create a pool of connections at start; borrow one; return to pool; reuse.</p>
<p>Replace “connection” with “thread”: same idea for jobs.</p>
<h3>02:05 — Why thread pools</h3>
<p>Creating a new thread for every independent job, running, then terminating → if 10 jobs, 10 threads → performance and memory problems.</p>
<p>Better: create a pool of e.g. 5 threads; submit jobs to the pool; threads reuse for multiple jobs (first 5 jobs, then remaining 5 by same threads).</p>
<p>Benefit: don’t create a separate thread per job; reuse → performance and memory improved.</p>
<h3>04:21 — Board: need of thread pool</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Creating a new thread for every job
// may create performance and memory problems
//
// To overcome this we should go for Thread pool
//
// Thread pool is a pool of already created threads
// ready to do our job
//
// Java 1.5 version introduces thread pool framework
// to implement thread pools
//
// Thread pool framework also known as Executor framework</pre>
<h3>06:39 — Creating a thread pool</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

ExecutorService service = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);
// pool of 3 threads (use 4, 10, ... as needed)</pre>
<h3>08:05 — Submit Runnable job; shutdown</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Runnable job:
class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        // job
    }
}

MyRunnable job = new MyRunnable();
service.submit(job);

// after jobs done:
service.shutdown();</pre>
<p>Board:</p>
<pre class='code'>// We can submit a Runnable job by using submit method
// We can shut down ExecutorService by using shutdown method</pre>
<p>Programmer defines jobs and submits; framework creates/starts/reuses threads.</p>
<h3>10:16 — Demo: PrintJob + ExecutorDemo</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

class PrintJob implements Runnable {
    String name;

    PrintJob(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void run() {
        System.out.println(name + &quot; job started by &quot;
                + Thread.currentThread().getName());
        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
        System.out.println(name + &quot; job completed by &quot;
                + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}

class ExecutorDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PrintJob[] jobs = {
            new PrintJob(&quot;Durga&quot;),
            new PrintJob(&quot;Ravi&quot;),
            new PrintJob(&quot;Shiva&quot;),
            new PrintJob(&quot;Pavan&quot;),
            new PrintJob(&quot;Suresh&quot;),
            new PrintJob(&quot;Anil&quot;)
        };

        ExecutorService service = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        for (PrintJob job : jobs) {
            service.submit(job);
        }
        service.shutdown();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Without executor: create 6 Runnable + 6 Thread + start — lots of code. With pool: framework takes care of create/start.</p>
<h3>13:45 — Live runs: pool size 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 with 6 jobs</h3>
<p>He changes newFixedThreadPool(n) and runs:</p>
<p>Key point: a single thread can be reused for multiple jobs.</p>
<h3>24:03 — Board restatement after copying program</h3>
<pre class='code'>// In the above example three threads are responsible
// to execute six jobs
// so that a single thread can be reused for multiple jobs</pre>
<h3>28:11 — Where thread pools are used</h3>
<p>Web / application servers: many requests; for each request a thread processes it; servers keep a pool of threads; after request, thread returns to pool.</p>
<p>Default pool size often ~60 (configurable in server console). While designing/developing web servers and application servers → thread pool concept.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board note:
// While developing web servers and application servers
// we can use thread pool concept</pre>
<h3>30:33 — Runnable limitation: no return value</h3>
<p>Normal approach:</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        // job
    }
}

MyRunnable r = new MyRunnable();
Thread t = new Thread(r);
t.start();
// after job, thread terminates; run() return type is void
// thread won&#x27;t return anything to the caller</pre>
<p>If after completing the job the thread must return something to the caller → use `Callable` instead of Runnable.</p>
<h3>32:21 — Callable vs Runnable (intro)</h3>
<p>Callable: after job, thread returns something. Runnable: after job, returns nothing.</p>
<p>Interview-important difference.</p>
<h3>34:27 — Submitting Callable; Future holds result</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.*;

ExecutorService service = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

for (Callable job : callableJobs) {
    Future f = service.submit(job); // hold returned value
    System.out.println(f.get());    // retrieve result
}
service.shutdown();</pre>
<p>Future object: hold / retrieve the return value of a Callable job.</p>
<h3>37:00 — Board: Callable and Future</h3>
<pre class='code'>// In the case of Runnable job
// thread won&#x27;t return anything after completing the job
//
// If a thread is required to return some result after execution
// then we should go for Callable
//
// Callable interface contains only one method: call
// public Object call() throws Exception
//
// If we submit a Callable object to executor
// then after completing the job
// thread returns an object of the type Future
//
// Future object can be used to retrieve the result
// from Callable job</pre>
<h3>41:04 — CallableFutureDemo: sum of first n numbers</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.*;

class MyCallable implements Callable {
    int num;

    MyCallable(int num) {
        this.num = num;
    }

    public Object call() throws Exception {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                + &quot; is responsible to find sum of first &quot; + num + &quot; numbers&quot;);
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= num; i++) {
            sum = sum + i;
        }
        return sum;
    }
}

class CallableFutureDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        MyCallable[] jobs = {
            new MyCallable(10),
            new MyCallable(20),
            new MyCallable(30),
            new MyCallable(40),
            new MyCallable(50),
            new MyCallable(60)
        };

        ExecutorService service = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);
        for (MyCallable job : jobs) {
            Future f = service.submit(job);
            System.out.println(f.get());
        }
        service.shutdown();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Sums (n(n+1)/2): he lists</p>
<pre class='code'>// prints (among thread messages) results:
// 55    (sum 1..10)
// 210   (sum 1..20)
// 465   (sum 1..30)
// 820   (sum 1..40)
// 1275  (sum 1..50)
// 1830  (sum 1..60)</pre>
<p>If thread returns something → Callable; if not → Runnable.</p>
<h3>50:24 — Differences between Runnable and Callable (full board)</h3>
<pre class='code'>// 1. If a thread is not required to return anything after completing the job
//      → go for Runnable
//    If a thread is required to return something after completing the job
//      → go for Callable
//
// 2. Runnable interface contains only one method: run
//    Callable interface contains only one method: call
//
// 3. Runnable job not required to return anything
//      → return type of run method is void
//    Callable job is required to return something
//      → return type of call method is Object
//
// 4. Within run method, if chance of checked exception
//      → compulsory handle by try-catch
//      → because we can&#x27;t use throws for run method
//        (parent Runnable.run doesn&#x27;t throw checked;
//         overriding rule)
//    Within call method, if chance of checked exception
//      → not required to handle by try-catch
//      → because call already throws Exception
//
// 5. Runnable present in java.lang package
//    Callable present in java.util.concurrent package
//
// 6. Runnable introduced in 1.0 version
//    Callable introduced in 1.5 version (with thread pools / Executor)</pre>
<p>Illustrating run vs throws:</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(1000); // checked → must try-catch
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
        // public void run() throws Exception { }  // CE: incompatible with Runnable.run
    }
}

class MyCallable implements Callable {
    public Object call() throws Exception {
        Thread.sleep(1000); // OK: call already throws Exception
        return &quot;ok&quot;;
    }
}</pre>
<h3>59:55 — Session wrap</h3>
<p>Covered: advantage of thread pools; how to create; Executor framework; need of Callable &amp; Future; differences Runnable vs Callable. Basic idea only (not full API encyclopedia).</p>
